# Loop Analysis

An automated insulin delivery system (Loop, AAPS, Trio, iAPS or OpenAPS) makes
small changes to your insulin all day. **Loop Analysis** shows these changes,
when they occurred, and what occurred after them. Use it to find answers to
questions such as these:

- _"Does my loop always add insulin at 3 in the morning?"_
- _"What does my glucose do after breakfast?"_
- _"How frequently does the loop not operate?"_

To open it, go to **Reports > Loop Analysis**. Anyone whose access includes
reports can open it. This includes members, guest links and apps that you
authorise. A public share link shows the pages, but it cannot show the figures,
because the share does not get the time zone.

<Callout type="warning" title="Observations, not advice">

  All of Loop Analysis is an observation of your own data. Each figure shows the
  number of days or events that it uses. No figure is an instruction. Speak to
  your diabetes care team before you change a setting.

</Callout>

## The five pages

Loop Analysis has five pages. The page switcher under the report header links
the pages. When you go to a different page, the range and the filters stay the
same.

| Page | The question that it answers |
|------|------------------------------|
| **Daily profile** | What does a usual day look like, hour by hour? When does the loop add or remove insulin? |
| **Meals and events** | What occurs after a meal, a correction, a rise with no carbs logged, or the start of exercise or a new sensor? |
| **Prediction accuracy** | How close are the predictions of the loop to the readings that followed them? |
| **Loop health** | How much of the time was the loop closed, and how frequently did it reach its limits? |
| **Settings evidence** | What does your data show about your basal rates, insulin sensitivity (ISF) and carb ratio? |

## The Filters panel

To change the range and the filters, open the **Filters** panel of the reports.
The Loop Analysis filters are under the date range.

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-filter-bar" />

- The date range: the days that the report uses. The maximum is 90 days. For a
  longer range, the report uses only the most recent 90 days, and it tells you
  this.
- **Compare with**: a second period to show with the first. Select
  **Previous period** for the period immediately before, or **Custom range** to
  select the dates. The charts show the comparison as dashed outlines. The Loop
  health tiles and the chart readouts show the compared value.
- **Profile**: use only the time when one profile was active.
- **Days of the week**: use only some days of the week, for example weekdays.
- **Exclude the time of**: remove the time of overrides, temporary targets,
  exercise, illness, or time not in closed loop. Illness and time not in closed
  loop are removed by default. The report removes only that time. It does not
  remove the full day.

**Time not in closed loop** uses the dates when each of your pumps was in use.
These dates are days in your time zone, from local midnight to local midnight.

The report always removes glucose readings that your data marks as not
reliable. Examples are compression lows (a false low when you lie on your
sensor) and sensor errors.

### Ranges that include today

A range never goes after the current time. If the range includes today, the
report uses today only up to now. When you compare with the previous period,
the comparison also stops at the same time of day on its last day.

### Time zone

Loop Analysis aligns days by the local time of day. It needs the time zone of
your data to do this. If it cannot get the time zone, each page shows
**This report needs a time zone** and the cause:

- **The profile settings do not have a time zone.** The owner of this data can
  set a time zone in the profile settings.
- **This server does not recognise the time zone in the profile settings.** The
  owner of this data can correct the time zone in the profile settings.
- **The time zone of this data did not load.** To try again, reload the report
  later.
- **A shared link does not show the time zone of this data.**

## Net loop correction

Many figures use **net loop correction**. This is the insulin that your loop
added above your scheduled basal, or removed from it. It is the sum of two
decisions of the algorithm:

- **Temp basals that the algorithm set**, measured against your scheduled
  basal. The schedule is adjusted for an override that changes your insulin
  needs. A temp above the schedule adds insulin. A temp below the schedule
  (also a zero temp) removes insulin.
- **Automatic boluses** (SMBs in AAPS, Trio, iAPS and OpenAPS, or the automatic
  boluses of Loop).

Many systems make most of their corrections with automatic boluses. So the
figure includes them. If it did not include them, it would show only a part of
what the loop does.

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-net-correction" />

Some insulin changes are not decisions of the algorithm. Net loop correction
does not include them. The Daily profile can show them as separate lines:

- temp basals that you set manually;
- pump suspends;
- delivery that the report calculated from the schedule (**Inferred**).

### Which temp basals the loop set

Trio, AAPS and OpenAPS do not record whether a temp basal came from the loop or
from you. Nocturne compares each temp with the decisions that your loop uploaded
near it:

- If the loop asked for the same rate within 5 minutes, the temp is from the
  algorithm.
- If the loop asked only for different rates, the temp is manual.
- If the loop uploaded no decisions near the temp, the temp keeps the origin
  that its uploader gives. This is the algorithm for a loop app, and manual for
  other sources.

The temp and the decision can come from devices with different names. For
example, AAPS can upload through Nightscout with no device name, and Trio
through Tidepool. Nocturne then uses the decisions of the one loop device that
uploaded near the temp. If two or more loop devices uploaded near the temp, the
temp keeps the origin that its uploader gives.

### Delivered basal

The report calculates delivered basal from the temp basal records of the pump.

- **Two sources for one pump.** Two sources can record the same pump, for
  example a loop and a Tidepool copy of it. The records of the loop are used
  first. The records of the other source fill only the time that the loop did
  not record.
- **A cancelled temp.** When a temp is cancelled, basal goes back to the
  schedule. The report uses the schedule for this time only when the rule for
  missing records applies.
- **Missing records.** If there is no record of delivery, the basal for that
  time is not known. The report uses the schedule only for a gap of 3 hours or
  less, with records on the two sides. A longer gap stays unknown.

## Read the charts

Most charts in Loop Analysis show a shaded band, not a single line.

- The **line** in the middle is the median. Half of your days (or readings) were
  above it and half were below it.
- The **darker band** shows the middle half (25th to 75th percentile).
- The **lighter band** shows most of your data (10th to 90th percentile).
- **n** is the number of days, readings or events that a figure uses.

The report measures two types of figure differently:

- For **glucose**, the report puts all the readings from all the days into each
  five-minute period. The AGP report does the same, so the bands agree with AGP.
  For glucose, n counts readings.
- **Rates** (basal, net loop correction, insulin on board and carbs on board)
  are calculated for each day first. Then the report compares the days. For
  rates, n counts days. A day counts in a period only when data covers half of the
  period or more.

If a figure uses **fewer than 5 days**, the chart shows it faded. There is not
enough data to show a pattern.

The report shows missing data as missing, never as zero. A zero temp is a real
zero, and it always counts.

### When parts are hidden

Your access can include only some types of data. Then some parts of the report
show **Hidden: your access does not include this data**. Each part needs access
to the type of data that it uses: glucose, treatments (insulin and carbs),
devices (the records of the loop and the pump) or therapy settings (your
profile). Filters that you cannot use are grey, and they show the access that
they need.

If you can see only the **last 24 hours**, the report shows only those 24 hours
for all ranges. It does not compare ranges. Settings evidence does not show. The
report tells you this.

## Daily profile

The Daily profile aligns your days by the time of day, in five-minute periods of
the local day. This shows the patterns at each time of day.

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-daily-profile" />

The tiles at the top show the totals:

- **Days with data**.
- **Total insulin** each day.
- **Basal / bolus**: the split between basal and bolus.
- **Automatic / manual bolus**.
- **Delivered basal ratio**: delivered basal divided by scheduled basal.
- **Net loop correction** each day.

Four tiles use only complete days: **Total insulin**, **Basal / bolus**,
**Automatic / manual bolus** and **Net loop correction**. A day counts in these
tiles only when the report uses 90% of the day or more. Delivery must also be
known for 90% of the day or more. Known delivery is recorded, or filled from the
schedule. Net loop correction each day also needs the schedule for 90% of the
day. The report never increases a part of a day to a full day. Today counts only when 90% of
the day is before now.

**Days with data** counts each day with glucose or treatments. **Delivered basal
ratio** adds all the days that have delivery and a schedule.

The charts are under the tiles. From top to bottom:

- **Glucose**, with your target range behind it.
- **Basal**: the scheduled basal and the delivered basal.
- **Net loop correction**: the insulin that the loop added or removed. A line
  shows the mean, with automatic boluses. A band shows the middle half of the
  temp basal change only. The chart under it shows the sum of the correction for
  each hour.
- **Insulin on board** and **carbs on board**.
- **Manual boluses** and **carbs**: how frequently and how much at each time of
  day.

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-daily-glucose" />

To list the days for a time, select the time or press Enter on it. Each day
links to **Day in Review** at that time. A day that the range includes only
partly, for example today, shows **Part of the day**.

## Meals and events

This page aligns all the events of one kind at the time that they occurred. Then
it shows what occurred after them: the change in glucose, glucose, the
correction of the loop, the insulin above your schedule since the event, and
insulin and carbs on board. You can compare a maximum of three kinds of event
(cohorts).

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-event-glucose-change" />

For each cohort, you can select a time of day. For meals, you can also select a
range of grams. Select the time to show before and after each event. The
default is 1 hour before and 5 hours after.

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-meals-events" />

The report uses only events whose full window is before now. The report does
not use an event until its window is complete.

### The events that you can select

- **Meals**: a carb entry. Entries at the same time count as one meal.
- **Correction boluses**: a manual bolus with no carbs logged in the 30 minutes
  before or after it. Automatic boluses are not correction boluses.
- **Unannounced rises**: a rise in glucose that looks like a meal, with no carbs
  logged. See below.
- **Sensor starts**, and the start of **exercise**, an **override** or a
  **temporary target**.

### How the report finds an unannounced rise

The report looks for the pattern that a meal makes:

1. **A stable floor.** Glucose stays stable for 30 minutes or more. It moves
   20 mg/dL (1.1 mmol/L) or less. The lowest quarter of its readings is at
   70 mg/dL (3.9 mmol/L) or more.
2. **A climb.** After the floor, glucose increases by 40 mg/dL (2.2 mmol/L) or
   more in 45 minutes. If glucose goes back to the floor, the climb starts
   again.
3. **Not a sensor error.** The report does not use a climb faster than
   5 mg/dL (0.3 mmol/L) each minute. It does not use a single jump of
   30 mg/dL (1.7 mmol/L) or more. It also does not use the shape of a
   **compression recovery**. In this shape, glucose decreases quickly. Then it
   increases to the value from before the decrease. This is the shape that you
   get when you lie on your sensor.
4. **No carbs logged.** If carbs were logged from 3 hours before the rise to
   30 minutes after it, or carbs were on board, the report does not count the rise.

A second climb within 2 hours is part of the same rise.

You can also select **Only rises with extra loop insulin**.
Then the report uses only rises where the loop added half an hour of your usual
basal or more, and 0.2 U or more, in the 45 minutes after the rise started. This
removes rises that your loop did not treat as real.

### Rises and falls

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-event-metrics" />

Each kind of event has a usual direction. After a meal, an unannounced rise or a
sensor start, the table shows rise figures. After a correction bolus, the start
of exercise, an override or a temporary target, the table shows fall figures.

For a rise, the table shows the **peak rise**, the **time to peak**, and the
time until glucose is again within 20 mg/dL (1.1 mmol/L) of the start glucose.

For a fall, the table shows these figures:

- **Drop to window end**: the start glucose minus the glucose at the end of the
  window. Settings evidence uses the same method over a maximum of 4 hours. The
  drop does not use the lowest reading, because noise can make the lowest
  reading too low.
- **Lowest glucose** and **Time to lowest glucose**.
- **Drop per unit above schedule**: the drop divided by the net insulin above
  your schedule. The insulin is for the full window of the event. This includes
  the time before the event. The table shows this figure only for isolated events with
  0.5 U or more above the schedule, and with no glucose below 70 mg/dL
  (3.9 mmol/L).
- **Went below range**: the number of events with a reading below 70 mg/dL
  (3.9 mmol/L) after the event.

### Isolated events

An event is **isolated** when no other meal, correction or meal-like rise occurs
in its window. To use only these events, select **Isolated events only**. If you
do not select it, the curves after a meal can also include the next snack.

The **observed grams per unit** is the grams of carbs for each unit of insulin
above your schedule in the window. It is not the same as your carb ratio, which
counts all of your bolus insulin. The figure shows only for isolated meals. When
a meal overlaps another event, the insulin in the window was not for that meal
only. So the grams divided by that insulin gives an incorrect value. The
figure also needs the delivery and the schedule for 90% of the window or more.

## Prediction accuracy

Your loop predicts your glucose, and it makes its decisions from these
predictions. This page compares the predictions with the readings that followed
them, at 30, 60, 90 and 120 minutes ahead.

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-predictions" />

- **Bias** is the average of _prediction minus reading_. A positive bias means
  that the predictions were too high. A negative bias means that they were too
  low.
- **MAE** (mean absolute error) is the average size of the error, in the two
  directions.
- **n** is the number of predictions that the report compared.

The report pairs a prediction only with a reading within 2.5 minutes of the
predicted time. If there is no reading that close, the prediction is unmatched.
The report does not use a horizon whose reading can still arrive.

### Why only 30 and 60 minutes are about your settings

A prediction assumes that nothing else will occur. But the loop continues to
change insulin after the prediction. After two hours, glucose also shows all of
these changes. So only the **bias at 30 and 60 minutes** gives information
about your settings. For example, if predictions after meals are usually too
high, speak to your diabetes care team. The longer horizons are for information only.

### Interrupted predictions

If you logged carbs or gave a manual bolus after a prediction and before its
horizon, the prediction did not know about it. These horizons are
**interrupted**. The report does not use them in a figure. Food that you did
not log does not interrupt a prediction.

### Curves and contexts

Loop makes one prediction. AAPS, Trio, iAPS and OpenAPS make a number of
predictions. Each one assumes something different: **IOB** (insulin only),
**zero temp** (if basal stops), **COB** (carbs absorb as entered) and **UAM**
(unannounced meal, from the rise in glucose). The report checks each curve
only in the contexts where the algorithm uses it. For example, IOB and zero
temp are checked after a correction or when fasting. COB is checked after a
meal.

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-prediction-detail" />

The **By hour and context** box shows the bias of one curve at each hour of the
day and in each context.

Two curves select the predictions that the report can check:

- The algorithm makes **UAM** only when glucose increases faster than insulin
  alone predicts, or when carbs are absorbing. So the report measures UAM
  accuracy only at these times.
- The algorithm removes the end of the **zero temp** curve where the curve
  increases above the target. So its long horizons come only from predictions
  that did not increase above the target.

If your access does not include treatments, the report hides the curves that
it checks only in some contexts: IOB, zero temp and COB. The page names these
curves.
The contexts come from treatments.

## Loop health

Loop health shows how the loop operated. Most tiles show a percentage of time.
**Loop gaps** and **Unrecorded delivery** show a count of periods.
**Requested, not done** shows a percentage of the changes that the loop asked
for. The last two tiles show the limits that your loop reported. The charts show
the data for each hour of the day.

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-loop-health" />

| Tile | What it shows |
|------|---------------|
| **Closed loop** | Time when the loop controlled basal delivery. |
| **CGM active** | Time when your sensor sent readings. |
| **Pump active** | Time when the loop uploaded a cycle. If there are no cycles, the report uses the delivery records. |
| **Loop gaps** | Gaps of more than 15 minutes between two loop cycles. |
| **Unrecorded delivery** | Periods when the loop operated but there is no record of the pump delivery. |
| **Algorithm zero temps** | Time when the loop set basal to zero, as a percentage of the time with known delivery. Suspends and manual zero temps are separate. |
| **At max basal** | Time when the temp basals of the loop were at the highest rate that it can set. |
| **At max IOB** | Time when insulin on board was at max IOB, as a percentage of loop time with a recorded reason (AAPS, Trio, iAPS, OpenAPS). |
| **Automatic boluses a day** | The number and the size of automatic boluses. |
| **Requested, not done** | Changes that the loop asked for but did not make, for example because the pump did not reply (AAPS, Trio, iAPS, OpenAPS). |
| **Max basal rate setting of the Loop app** and **Max IOB** | The limits that your loop reported. |

### At max basal and "max basal known"

For **Loop**, a temp is at its limit when it is at your maximum basal rate
setting. For **AAPS, Trio, iAPS and OpenAPS**, the limit is a safety limit that
the algorithm calculates each time. Only the reason that the loop gives tells
when the temp was at that limit. **Max basal known** is the percentage of the temp
time of the algorithm where Nocturne can tell. **At max basal** uses only that
time.

### Unrecorded delivery

When the loop operates but there is no record of the pump delivery, the basal
for that time is not known. Loop Analysis uses your schedule only for a gap of
3 hours or less, with records on the two sides. A large quantity of unrecorded
delivery usually means that pump history does not arrive in Nocturne. It also
makes the insulin figures on the other pages less complete.

### When a tile shows "Unknown"

When the report cannot calculate a tile, the tile shows **Unknown** and the
reason:

- **No loop cycles in this range**: the loop recorded nothing.
- **The system that ran the loop does not record this**: for example, Loop does
  not record a maximum IOB.
- **Only the Loop app uploads this setting**: the maximum basal rate setting.
- **No loop cycle in this range recorded the data that this figure needs**: the
  loop operated, but it did not record the reason that the figure uses.
- **No loop cycle in this range set the temp basals that this figure needs**: no
  loop decision matched the temps.
- **All the data for this figure is in time that the filters exclude.**
- **The active profile does not record a maximum basal rate.**
- **The loop did not set a temp basal in this range.**
- **Nothing measured in this range.**

**Max IOB** shows only when the loop reports that max IOB limited a dose.

## Settings evidence

Settings evidence compares your profile with your data, for three settings:

- **Basal**, in two-hour blocks of the day.
- **Insulin sensitivity (ISF)**, also known as the correction factor, and
  **carb ratio**, in four blocks: overnight
  (midnight to 6 am), morning (6 am to 11 am), midday (11 am to 5 pm) and evening
  (5 pm to midnight).

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-settings-evidence" />

Each figure shows the number of days or events that it uses, their spread, a
confidence level, and a list of all the days or events. A hollow dot shows a
figure with low confidence. To see more, open a block. It shows the concerns,
how many days or events the report did not use and why, and the days or events
that it used.

<Callout type="warning" title="Speak to your diabetes care team before you change a setting">

  These figures are observations of your own data. They are not
  recommendations. A figure can look correct and be incorrect because of
  something that the data does not show. Speak to your diabetes care team before you
  change a setting.

</Callout>

### How the report calculates each figure

**Basal.** For each two-hour block on each day, the report estimates the basal
rate that would keep glucose stable. It starts from your scheduled rate. It
adds the insulin that the loop added or removed. It uses an ISF to adjust for
glucose that went up or down across the block. This ISF comes from your data
when the report has a confident one. If not, it comes from your profile, adjusted by how your data compares with your profile in the other blocks. The
figure is the average of the days that the report can use.

The figure shows only when 70% of these days or more agree on one direction:
the loop added insulin, or it removed insulin. A day where the loop changed
delivery by less than 10% of the schedule agrees with the two directions. The
figure does not show when glucose increased or decreased by more than
10 mg/dL (0.6 mmol/L) an hour on average.

**The report counts the insulin that acted in each block.** The report counts
the insulin that the loop added in the block where the pump delivered it. It
then adds the insulin that was active at the start of the block. It removes the
insulin that was still active at the end. So each block counts the insulin that
acted in it. The blocks are two hours long because the insulin of the loop acts
over the next one or two hours.

**Insulin sensitivity (ISF).** The report follows each correction bolus for 4 hours. If
you log carbs 3 to 4 hours after the bolus, the window stops at the carbs. The
figure is the decrease in glucose divided by the insulin that acted. The page
shows the median of the corrections in each block. The report uses the reading
at the end of the window, not the lowest reading. The lowest of many readings is
usually a little too low.

**Carb ratio.** The report follows each meal from 15 minutes before it (to
include a bolus before the meal) to 4 hours after it. The figure is the grams
eaten divided by the insulin that the meal needed. This is the insulin that
acted, adjusted for the change between the start glucose and the end glucose.
The page shows the median of the meals in each block.

The ISF and the carb ratio measure insulin above your
**observed** basal, when the report has a confident basal figure for that time.
If not, they use your schedule, and the figure gets a concern. If your scheduled
basal is too low, the loop adds insulin. That added insulin is not for the meal
or the correction.

### What the report does not use

The report does not use a day or an event if one of these conditions occurs in
its window. Each day or event counts once, under the first condition that it
fails. The page shows how many it did not use for each reason.

**Basal blocks:**

- The loop was open, or an override, temporary target, exercise or illness was
  active.
- Carbs were logged in the 4 hours before the block or during it, or carbs were
  on board.
- Glucose increased as it does after a meal, in the 4 hours before the block or
  during it.
- A manual bolus occurred in the 5 hours before the block or during it.
- A manual temp basal or a suspend occurred.
- Delivery records cover less than 90% of the block.
- Basal delivery was not recorded and was different from the schedule.
- Insulin on board is not known at the start or the end of the block.
- The insulin on board at the start and the end comes from different sources.
- There is no reading near the start or the end of the block.
- The block is outside your filters.

**Corrections:**

- Carbs were logged from 1 hour before to 3 hours after.
- Another meal, correction or meal-like rise occurred in the window.
- An override, temporary target, exercise or illness was active.
- No target range was set.
- Glucose was not above your target range, or it moved more than
  15 mg/dL (0.8 mmol/L) in the 15 minutes before.
- The insulin on board was already more than the bolus, or it was negative by
  more than the bolus.
- Glucose went below 70 mg/dL (3.9 mmol/L) in the window.
- Less than 0.5 U of insulin acted.
- Readings, delivery records or insulin on board are missing, or the insulin on
  board at the start and the end comes from different sources.
- The window is outside your filters, or not fully in the range.

**Meals:**

- Another meal, correction or meal-like rise occurred in the window.
- An override, temporary target, exercise or illness was active.
- Glucose at the start was below 70 mg/dL or above 180 mg/dL (3.9 or
  10.0 mmol/L), or it moved more than 15 mg/dL (0.8 mmol/L) in the 15 minutes
  before the meal.
- The meal needed less than 0.5 U of insulin.
- Readings, delivery records or insulin on board are missing, or the insulin on
  board at the start and the end comes from different sources.
- The window is outside your filters, or not fully in the range.

The page shows a figure only when it has 5 days or more (basal) or 3 events or
more (ISF and carb ratio).

### Confidence

Each figure has **High**, **Moderate** or **Low** confidence. A figure with no
concerns has High confidence. One concern gives Moderate, and two or more give
Low. A figure from fewer than 6 days or events always has Low confidence. High
confidence means that the report found none of these concerns. It does not mean
that the figure is correct. These are the concerns:

- Fewer than 10 days or events give the figure.
- The days or events are very different from each other: the middle half of
  the values is wider than half of the figure.
- The report used the ISF of your profile, not an ISF from your data, to convert
  changes in glucose. The converted change was 15% or more of the figure.
- The report measured insulin against a basal that it did not check for this
  time of day.
- Much of the insulin was still active at the end of the window.
- More than half of the events overlapped other meals, boluses or rises.
- Fewer than one day in five has carb entries. So the fasting time comes from
  glucose only.
- Glucose usually still changed at the end of the window.
- The decrease in insulin on board from the start to the end of the block was
  15% or more of the basal figure.
- 15% or more of the corrections went below 70 mg/dL (3.9 mmol/L), so the
  report did not use them. So the ISF can show less effect for each unit than
  the true value.

### Why figures for more insulin need more evidence

Some figures point to **more insulin** than your profile by 10% or more: a
higher basal, a lower ISF, or a lower carb ratio. The 10% compares
the rounded figure that the page shows with your profile. If 15% or more of such
a figure comes from another estimate, its confidence decreases by one level.
Other estimates are the ISF that converts glucose changes, the basal that the
report measures insulin above, and the insulin on board at the start and the
end of the window. Many corrections that went low have the same effect on an
ISF. The page shows this as a concern. An error in this direction can cause a low.
So the report needs stronger evidence for it.

## How it is different from the Nightscout Loopalyzer

Loop Analysis replaces the Loopalyzer report of Nightscout. It corrects the
problems that made Loopalyzer difficult to trust:

- **Zeros count.** Loopalyzer did not use zeros in its averages. So the loop
  correction looked larger than it was.
- **Automatic boluses count.** Loopalyzer showed only temp basals. If your loop
  corrects with SMBs, Loopalyzer showed only a part of what it did.
- **Daylight saving time is correct.** Loopalyzer did not use days that were not
  exactly 24 hours long. After a change, it showed glucose with an error of one
  hour.
- **Readings exactly on a five-minute mark stay in.** One missing basal record
  does not move all the times after it.
- **The spread and n are shown**, not only an average.
- **Totals**: insulin each day, with basal, automatic boluses and manual boluses.
- **A maximum of 90 days**, not 14.
- **Meals align correctly.** Loopalyzer moved the time from the first meal in the
  data, not from the first meal in the day. It also lost data that moved past
  midnight.
- **You can remove time**: overrides, temporary targets, exercise, illness and
  time not in closed loop.
